<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Base Info.plist for the PeakLog app target.

  Everything else is still generated by Xcode (GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE = YES);
  the generated keys are merged on top of this file. It exists only to carry
  keys Xcode's INFOPLIST_KEY_* mechanism refuses to pass through — that
  mechanism only honors keys it already knows about, and these are ours.

  Both values are build settings, expanded at build time. Leave them unset and
  they expand to an empty string, which SupabaseConfig reads as "not
  configured" and falls back to the values compiled into the binary — so a
  build that knows nothing about them keeps working exactly as before.

  To point a build at a different Supabase project (i.e. to rotate):
      xcodebuild … PEAKLOG_SUPABASE_URL=https://<ref>.supabase.co \
                   PEAKLOG_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_…
  or set the same two settings in an xcconfig.

  The publishable key is not a secret — it is meant to be readable in a client
  binary, and row access is enforced by RLS. This seam is about rotation, not
  concealment.

  This file lives outside PeakLog/ on purpose: that directory is a synchronized
  group, so a plist placed there would also be copied into the bundle as a
  resource and collide with the Info.plist being generated.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>PeakLogSupabaseURL</key>
	<string>$(PEAKLOG_SUPABASE_URL)</string>
	<key>PeakLogSupabasePublishableKey</key>
	<string>$(PEAKLOG_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY)</string>
</dict>
</plist>
